y learn too that the silent  final E is added to let you know that  this s is not a suffix it is a part of  the basic word and then their last one  is called their last rule that they  learn is called odd job numbers six  which means that E is doing a number of  odd little things and it occurs less  often it might have been an e that was  there in a pronunciation long ago it  might be an e that's there to  differentiate between homophone  homophones it might be an e that's there  to extend a word they're all just sort  of little less specific rules that fall  there these first five are the ones that  they know well so you need to know well  so if you're working on spelling words  with them you can understand what's  happening there and there's one more a  little bit tricky rule that students  have learned and it's called the two-way  marking what they learn is that some  words are said one way in speech but  then they're said another way to spell  them and the best example I can give of  that for you as an adult is the word  Wednesday a lot of us learned that to  set it Wednesday  but we spelled it wet nest day so we  learned a funny way to pronounce it to  spell it so here are just a couple  examples of that the word is pronounced  in speech as again but students will  learn and it's it is a two-way word and  they will pronounce it a gain and then  they will mark it with that funny speech  then they'll go back because that's not  the way we we talk they'll go back and  they'll add a little cap above the two  parts of that word that were pronounced  in a different way here's another  example like that we say said but  students will learn to memorize and to  pronounce this as fade and then they  know this two letter phone again  phonogram a two letter a that we do not  use at the end of English words gets an  underline and because of that funny  change that we made to pronounce it it's  going to also get a little cap at the  top so all things that your student has  s